# Niagara source-only emitter compile-owner checkpoint

## Scope

This checkpoint changes Custom HLSL replacement verification in the
UnrealMCP editor plugin, its source contract test, and the corresponding tool
documentation, plus the explicit-root path-resolution correction needed for
independent checkout verification. It does not deliver the unfinished CubeFluidNinjaLive levels,
reference assets, generated fixtures, or the other local authoring extensions.

## Cause and correction

UE 5.6.1 `UNiagaraScript::IsCompilable` excludes EmitterSpawnScript and
EmitterUpdateScript. Their source is compiled through SystemSpawnScript and
SystemUpdateScript respectively. Requiring standalone cached VM executable IDs
for these source-only scripts made successful compilation fail the save gate.

The handler records source-only invalidations separately and requires each
corresponding system owner to appear in the actually invalidated executable
set. Every affected executable must still show changed cached ID, stable
post-edit input ID, exact applied ID, completed compilation and no errors.
Missing owner coverage fails closed. Shared snapshot collection and other
commands are unchanged. No project runtime API or MCP content dependency is
introduced.

The path helper now honors an explicit `repo_root` before consulting whether
a historical absolute path exists on this host. Otherwise a still-existing
primary checkout can silently take precedence over the requested audit root.
Traversal and unrelated absolute-path handling are unchanged. Existing path
portability and readiness checks reproduced this issue in the isolated worktree
and passed with the correction.

## Evidence and limits

The original working-tree investigation passed UE 5.6.1 Editor build, full
42/42 offline checks with 8,980 unchanged input hashes, and live bridge 6/6.
A disposable independently authored fixture verified both owner mappings,
successful saving, deliberate compile-error save blocking, unchanged disk
bytes on that failure, and a separately reconciled corrective write.

An independent reviewer found no P0/P1 defect. Explicit missing-owner and
cached-ID-mismatch fault injection remains future regression coverage; source
contracts are not represented as behavioral proof of those branches.

The working-tree evidence included additional local changes and therefore does
not by itself identify the isolated commit candidate. The submit workflow also
checks the selected patch on a separate worktree of current main. The final
commit/push handoff records those results separately.

The study's CPU `Reader.Num` output-lvalue workaround and the array runtime
integration remain outside this narrow commit. Full Tutorial01 implementation,
array CPU/GPU runtime verification, final content independence and visual
acceptance are not claimed.
